{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791192690,"nonce":"O5Qf86gdD29nHs8lUtQgZIh85yE=","message":"Follow-up: how built-in elements can share Div's plumbing. Spiked in scratch\nfiles (not in the repo); results are measured, the plan is not decided.\n\n\n- Element (44 lines) owns a Node and a tree, and nothing else: appendChild,\n  remove, owner cleanup. It carries NO property API.\n- The plumbing lives on Node (353 lines): set(Prop\u003cT\u003e, IntoReactive\u003cT\u003e), the\n  stylesheet writer, ownership, unset, transitions. Div, Text, Svg and every\n  component share it by HAVING one, not by inheriting from Div.\n- Text is 69 lines and Svg 44. Neither has width, bg, padding or class of its\n  own. Each creates a node of its kind, registers a CSS identity (its tag)\n  and takes two or three attributes. Everything else reaches it through\n  classes, CSS, tw() and hxx, all of which end in node.set.\n- Component\u003cProps\u003e extends Element: \"the component is that element, not a\n  box around it\". Its render builds a Div from an hxx template. About 100\n  components are 5.7k lines of Haxe plus 1,035 lines of CSS, with no style\n  builder code in them.\n- Inheritance is used only where behaviour really is-a: AlertDialog extends\n  Dialog, ContextMenu extends DropdownMenu, For and Show extend Div.\n- ashui's own residue: a property still appears in about five places\n  (PropertyId, Prop, DivAttributes and its constructor, the CSS handler,\n  the router). Div's constructor repeats an if-not-null node.set per\n  attribute by hand. So ashui did not reach one place either.\n\n\nDiv has 55 fields and about 287 builder methods. The other elements carry\npartial copies: Text has no background, opacity or event handlers, which is\nwhy text(..).p(1) does not compile. Counting methods that share a name with\na Div method: Notch 73 of 119, Image 30 of 70, Svg 25 of 36, Text 20 of 62,\nCanvas 14 of 17, RichText 13 of 40, and Stack 127 of 127 by forwarding to an\ninner Div. Same name is not same behaviour, so this is an upper bound on\ntrue duplicates. Two shapes: re-derive against the element's own fields, or\nforward to an inner Div. Separately, 36 ElementBuilder impls hand-write 337\ntrait methods between them (median 9 of 35), and forgetting one silently\ndrops behaviour, which is the forwarding landmine already in memory.\n\n\n- Deref to Div: does not compile (E0507). Builder methods take self by\n  value and Deref only lends.\n- A trait with default methods over an accessor to a shared Core: compiles,\n  keeps the concrete type through a chain, mixes shared and element-specific\n  methods freely.\n- One generic El\u003cK\u003e over a kind marker, with type aliases Div and Text:\n  compiles, and callers still write -\u003e Div.\n- Redefining a shared method for one kind (inherent on El\u003cK\u003e and on\n  El\u003cTextKind\u003e): rejected (E0592). Per-kind behaviour needs a hook, which is\n  how ashui records a text measurement change on a font-size write.\n- A single property table in a macro_rules: one row generates the\n  PropertyId variant, the CSS name, the resolved field, a typed builder\n  accepting a constant or a signal, and the single writer that CSS reuses.\n  Compiles.\n- Coexistence: with the shared surface as a blanket trait, an element's own\n  inherent method wins, the generated one fills the gaps, and a Div can\n  carry none of its own. So elements migrate one at a time and the compiler\n  keeps working throughout.\n\n\nMake the shared thing a Core (ashui's Node) that every element owns, expose\nthe builder surface as ONE macro-generated trait over HasCore, and generate\nit from the same property table that produces PropertyId, the CSS names and\nthe writer. Prefer the trait over El\u003cK\u003e for the first step: it is\nincremental, where El\u003cK\u003e is all-or-nothing per type and rewrites the widget\nwrappers (Stateful, Scroll, Motion) that hold a Div.\n\nRisks and costs, not tested:\n- The trait must be in scope at every call site. Inside blinc_layout that is\n  a very large number of files, so Div keeps its inherent methods until last.\n- A blanket impl can collide with a method of the same name on another\n  trait. ElementBuilder has element_id and children_builders; a builder\n  named id or class needs checking.\n- Compile errors and rustdoc name the trait methods, not the element.\n- Monomorphisation: keep each generated body a thin shell over a\n  non-generic Core method.\n- The 337 hand-written ElementBuilder methods are a second surface. A derive\n  that forwards the core-derived ones removes the forgetting bug, but it is\n  separate work and not shown here.\n\nRelated: 6f7b001 steps 1 and 2 (the property channel and CSS through it).\nThis is the same table, seen from the element side.","files":null}]}